  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/Seythu Muhammed vs State Of Kerala on 18 June, 2025.json",
  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Seythu Muhammed vs State Of Kerala on 18 June, 2025 Author: Kauser Edappagath Bench: Kauser Edappagath Crl.R.P.No.343/2011 1 2025:Ker:43468 In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Dr. Justice Kauser Edappagath Wednesday, The 18Th Day Of June 2025 / 28Th Jyaishta, 1947 Crl.Rev.Pet No. 343 Of 2011 Against The Judgment Dated 10.01.2011 In Crl.A No.508 Of 2009 Of Additional Sessions Court, Alappuzha.",
    "facts": "The petitioner is the 1st accused in C.C.No.244/2006 on the files of the Judicial First Class Magistrate Court-I, Alappuzha (for short, the trial court). The offences alleged are punishable under Sections 2(ix)(k), 7(ii) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (for short, the PFA Act) and Rules 32 and 50 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955 (for short, the PFA Rules) and Rule 10 of the KPFA Rules punishable under Section 16(1)(a) of the PFA Act. 2. The prosecution case in short is that on 8.12.2004 at about 10.30 am, the accused sold three packets of wheat powder each weighing 500 gms to the complainant. After analysis by the Public Analyst, he opined that the sample is mis- branded. 3. PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts. P1 to P20 were marked on the side of the prosecution. No defence evidence was adduced. The petitioner challenged the conviction and sentence of the trial court before the Additional Sessions Court, Alappuzha (for short, the appellate court) in Crl.A.No.508 of 2009. The appellate court dismissed the appeal. This revision petition has been filed challenging the concurrent finding of the conviction and sentence of both the trial court as well as the appellate court.",
    "arguments": "4. The learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that admittedly, the petitioner is not the proprietor or licensee and that as per the prosecution case, he is only a sales man and hence he could not have been convicted. The learned counsel relying on few documents produced before this Court in Crl.M.A No.6937/2016 submitted that those documents would show that the 2nd accused is the licensee and hence the conviction of the petitioner is bad. On the other hand, the learned Prosecutor supported the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence and argued that the necessary ingredients of the offences charged have been established by the prosecution beyond reasonable doubt. The learned Prosecutor further submitted that the documents produced by the petitioner at this stage cannot be entertained.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4. The learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that admittedly, the petitioner is not the proprietor or licensee and that as per the prosecution case, he is only a sales man and hence he could not have been convicted. The learned counsel relying on few documents produced before this Court in Crl.M.A No.6937/2016 submitted that those documents would show that the 2nd accused is the licensee and hence the conviction of the petitioner is bad.",
    "respondent_arguments": "On the other hand, the learned Prosecutor supported the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence and argued that the necessary ingredients of the offences charged have been established by the prosecution beyond reasonable doubt. The learned Prosecutor further submitted that the documents produced by the petitioner at this stage cannot be entertained.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
